g†blog.ap.org cite175†AP Images Spotlight Blog†apimagesblog.com L2022: \nL2023: Copyright 2026 The Associated Press. All Rights Reserved.\nL2024: \nL2025:   * cite158†twitter†twitter.com L2026:   * cite159†instagram†www.instagram.com L2027:   * cite160†facebook†www.facebook.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMachine learning - Latest research and news | Nature (https://www.nature.com/subjects/machine-learning)\nciteturn924view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/subjects/machine-learning\",\"lineno\":60}); Total lines: 241\nL0:   * cite0†View all journals L1:   * cite1†Saved research L2:   * cite2†Search L3:   * cite3†Account†my-profile.springernature.com cite4†Log in†idp.nature.com L4: # Machine learning articles from across Nature Portfolio\nL5: \nL6:   * cite5†Atom L7:   * cite6†RSS Feed L8: \nL9: [Button: Definition]\nL10: \nL11: Machine learning is the ability of a machine to improve its performance based on previous results. Machine learning methods enable computers to learn without being explicitly programmed and have multiple applications, for example, in the improvement of data mining algorithms.\nL12: \nL13: ## Featured\nL14: \nL15:   * cite7†Image†media.springernature.com L16: ### cite8†Turning scientific research papers into interactive AI agents L17: \nL18: Scientific knowledge is mostly stored in static papers. An automated framework called Paper2Agent can now transform each paper into an active artificial intelligence agent — a virtual corresponding author that answers questions, applies the paper’s methods to new data, and collaborates with other paper agents. This makes research easier to reproduce, reuse and extend.\nL19: \nL20: News & Views 16 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L21:   * cite10†Image†media.springernature.com L22: ### cite11†When pathology segmentation learns to listen L23: \nL24: A natural-language-guided pathology segmentation model is developed to link pathological language with pathology image content to produce semantic masks, offering a path toward computational pathology systems that are more flexible, interpretable and aligned with human expertise.\nL25: \nL26:     * Wei Shen\nL27: \nL28: News & Views 10 Sept 2026 cite12†Nature Computational Science L29: \nL30: Volume: 6, P: 917-918\nL31: \nL32:   * cite13†Image†media.springernature.com L33: ### cite14†Steering machine reasoning with brain signals L34: \nL35: Representational alignment can reveal similarities between human brain activity and language models. Work now demonstrates that it can also guide learning, improving the reliability of artificial reasoning.\nL36: \nL37:     * Changde Du\nL38:     * Huiguang He\nL39: \nL40: News & Views 01 Sept 2026 cite15†Nature Machine Intelligence L41: \nL42: Volume: 8, P: 1331-1332\nL43: ## Latest Research and Reviews\nL44: \nL45:   * ### cite16†Bayesian bilevel operator learning with low-rank adaptation for efficient uncertainty quantification of PDE inverse problems L46: \nL47: Estimating parameters of partial differential equations from data with quantified uncertainty is computationally demanding. Here, authors combine bilevel local operator learning with low rank adaptation to enable efficient gradient-based posterior sampling without adjoint equations, pretraining data, or sampling network weights.\nL48:     * Ray Zirui Zhang\nL49:     * Christopher E. Miles\nL50:     * John S. Lowengrub\nL51: \nL52: Research Open Access 19 Sept 2026 cite17†Nature Communications L53: \nL54:   * ### cite18†ReScale4DL: balancing pixel and contextual information for enhanced bioimage segmentation L55: Deep-learning segmentation accuracy depends on image resolution, with each architecture having an optimum, usually at a lower microscopy resolution. Here, the authors present ReScale4DL, a framework for optimising imaging experiments by selecting resolutions to balance accuracy, throughput and cost.\nL56: \nL57:     * Mariana G. Ferreira\nL58:     * Bruno M. Saraiva\nL59:     * Estibaliz Gómez-de-Mariscal\nL60: \nL61: Research Open Access 18 Sept 2026 cite17†Nature Communications L62: \nL63:   * cite19†Image†media.springernature.com L64: ### cite20†ConvexGating infers gating strategies from clusters in single cell cytometry data L65: \nL66: Here, the authors develop ConvexGating, an AI tool that learns interpretable, data-driven gating strategies for cell sorting. They show that when analyzing scRNA-seq data, it yields low-contamination populations and also works across flow cytometry, cyTOF and CITE-seq data.\nL67: \nL68:     * Vincent D. Friedrich\nL69:     * Karola Mai\nL70:     * Maren Büttner\nL71: \nL72: Research Open Access 18 Sept 2026 cite17†Nature Communications L73: Volume: 17, P: 9975\nL74: \nL75:   * ### cite21†Advanced quantitative mapping of Alzheimer’s disease neuropathology and microglial activation in post-mortem hippocampal tissue L76: \nL77:     * Terri-Leigh Stephen\nL78:     * Laura Korobkova\nL79:     * Michael S. Bienkowski\nL80: \nL81: Research Open Access 17 Sept 2026 cite22†Scientific Reports L82: \nL83:   * ### cite23†Exploring the mitochondrial landscape in trabecular meshwork of primary open-angle glaucoma for novel therapeutic targets L84: \nL85:     * Gan Min\nL86:     * Long Bo\nL87:     * Li Kaiming\nL88: Research Open Access 16 Sept 2026 cite22†Scientific Reports L89: \nL90:   * ### cite24†Deep learning coupled with scalable domain-specific structural validation expands RNA virus discovery from metatranscriptomes L91: \nL92: Here, the authors develop Rider, a method that integrates protein language modelling with domain-specific structural validation to detect divergent RNA viruses across more than 10,000 metatranscriptomes, including long polyproteins and short fragments.\nL93: \nL94:     * Gaoyang Luo\nL95:     * Zelin Zang\nL96:     * Feng Ju\nL97: Research Open Access 16 Sept 2026 cite17†Nature Communications L98: \nL99: cite25†All Research & Reviews L100: ## News and Comment\nL101: \nL102:   * cite26†Image†media.springernature.com L103: \nL104: ### cite27†AI cracked the Navier–Stokes challenge. What does that mean for physics? L105: \nL106: Physicists and mathematicians are going beyond the classic equations of fluid dynamics to understand turbulence — often with the help of AI.\nL107: \nL108:     * Nicola Jones\nL109: \nL110: News 18 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L111: \nL112:   * cite28†Image†media.springernature.com L113: ### cite29†How fast are you ageing? Ask AI L114: \nL115: A new system will help scientists to refine large language models for longevity research and clarify ‘biological’ age.\nL116: \nL117:     * Heidi Ledford\nL118: \nL119: News 17 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L120: \nL121:   * cite30†Image†media.springernature.com L122: ### cite31†How a team of AIs discovered a promising lung-cancer drug L123: \nL124: Researchers developed a ‘virtual biotech’ made up of as many as 37,000 agents reporting to an 'chief scientist'.\nL125: \nL126:     * Ewen Callaway\nL127: \nL128: News 17 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L129: \nL130:   * cite32†Image†media.springernature.com L131: ### cite33†AI companies must work with the research community to protect attribution L132: \nL133: An era-defining mathematics claim raises questions over how AI tools credit earlier work.\nL134: \nL135: Editorial 16 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L136: \nL137: Volume: 657, P: 573\nL138: \nL139:   * cite7†Image†media.springernature.com L140: ### cite8†Turning scientific research papers into interactive AI agents L141: \nL142: Scientific knowledge is mostly stored in static papers. An automated framework called Paper2Agent can now transform each paper into an active artificial intelligence agent — a virtual corresponding author that answers questions, applies the paper’s methods to new data, and collaborates with other paper agents. This makes research easier to reproduce, reuse and extend.\nL143: \nL144: News & Views 16 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L145:   * cite34†Image†media.springernature.com L146: ### cite35†AI tool turns any paper into an ‘agent’ that can collaborate and answer complex queries L147: \nL148: The Paper2Agent system makes it easier for researchers to reproduce papers and understand work in unfamiliar fields, authors say.\nL149: \nL150:     * Kaia Glickman\nL151: \nL152: News 16 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature L153: \nL154: cite36†All News & Comment L155: \nL156: ## Search\nL157: \nL158: Search articles by subject, keyword or author\nL159: \nL160: [Input]\nL161: \nL162: Show results from [Select]\nL163: \nL164: [Button: Search]\nL165: \nL166: cite37†Advanced search L167: ### Quick links\nL168: \nL169:   * cite38†Explore articles by subject L170:   * cite39†Find a job L171:   * cite40†Guide to authors L172:   * cite41†Editorial policies L173: \nL174: Nature.com\nL175: \nL176: ## nature.com footer links\nL177: \nL178: ### About Nature Portfolio\nL179: \nL180:   * cite42†About us L181:   * cite43†Press releases L182:   * cite44†Press office†press.nature.com L183:   * cite45†Contact us†support.nature.com L184: \nL185: ### Discover content\nL186: \nL187:   * cite0†Journals A-Z L188:   * cite38†Articles by subject L189:   * cite46†protocols.io†www.protocols.io L190:   * cite47†Nature Index†www.natureindex.com L191: ### Publishing policies\nL192: \nL193:   * cite48†Nature portfolio policies L194:   * cite49†Open access L195: \nL196: ### Author & Researcher services\nL197: \nL198:   * cite50†Reprints & permissions L199:   * cite51†Research data†www.springernature.com L200:   * cite52†Language editing†authorservices.springernature.com L201:   * cite53†Scientific editing†authorservices.springernature.com L202:   * cite54†Nature Masterclasses†masterclasses.nature.com L203:   * cite5